## Chapter One: The Protocol That Trusted Too Much

It started, as these things often do, with a researcher poking at something everyone else had assumed was fine.

The thing they poked was called MCP — the Model Context Protocol. If you haven't met it yet, think of it as the USB port of the AI world. It's the standard way an AI model plugs into your files, your databases, your tools, your ticketing system. It's the plumbing that lets an assistant actually *do* things instead of just talk about them.

In April 2026, a security firm called OX Security published what they'd found. Their report, published April 15, 2026, and dubbed "The Mother of All AI Supply Chains," identified four distinct exploitation families and more than 10 CVEs rated high or critical across widely deployed AI frameworks including LiteLLM, LangFlow, Flowise, Windsurf, and Langchain-Chatchat [reference:0][reference:1].

Buried inside MCP's STDIO transport — the mechanism that lets a local process talk to an AI model — was a flaw. Not a small one. A crafted input could ride through that channel and land as a system command. Not "read a file." Not "leak a token." *Execute whatever I tell you.*

The number that made people sit up: over 200,000 vulnerable instances. Over 32,000 dependent repositories. All of them built on top of a protocol that, in this specific configuration, would pass along instructions it should never have passed along [reference:2].

Then came the part that mattered even more than the bug.

OX Security coordinated with Anthropic. And Anthropic's answer, according to the disclosure, was that the behavior was **by design**. Sanitizing input, they said, is the developer's job. On January 7, 2026, researchers at OX Security contacted Anthropic with evidence that the STDIO interface allowed execution of arbitrary operating system commands on any host running an MCP implementation. Anthropic's response characterized the behavior as expected. Nine days later, Anthropic updated its SECURITY.md to note that STDIO adapters should be used with caution, but made no architectural changes to the SDKs [reference:3].

So the vendors didn't patch it. Anthropic updated its own security documentation instead, adding a quiet little line telling developers that STDIO adapters should be "used with caution." That's it. That was the fix. A sentence in a doc [reference:4].

**Why it should bother you:** every MCP server in your environment is now part of your attack surface. If a protocol's designers consider command injection to be *your* problem, then it is, in fact, your problem. Log the tool calls. Lock down the adapters. Treat MCP config like you treat firewall rules.

