## Chapter Five: Agentjacking, or How an Error Message Became Remote Code Execution

Last one. And it's the one that should make you check your developer endpoints tomorrow morning.

June 2026. A firm called Tenet Security disclosed an entirely new attack class, and they gave it a name: **Agentjacking**. Researchers disclosed "agentjacking" on June 3, 2026: a class of attacks that weaponize Sentry's open error-ingest architecture to inject malicious instructions into AI coding agents via the Model Context Protocol (MCP) [reference:14].

Here's the setup. Developers use AI coding agents like Claude Code. Those agents use MCP to reach out to tools like Sentry — the error-monitoring platform that tells you when your app is breaking.

And here's the assumption everyone was making: the agent trusts what Sentry tells it. Of course it does. Sentry is *your* tool.

So Tenet Security crafted a malicious error event. They injected it into a public Sentry DSN — the client-side key that apps use to report crashes. Tenet tested 100-plus targets in controlled conditions and achieved an 85% success rate. The agent ran the attacker's code with the developer's full privileges, and not one alert fired. EDR, WAF, IAM, and the firewall all missed it completely [reference:15].

That's the whole chain. A fake crash report becomes remote code execution, because the AI in the middle trusted its input.

Tenet disclosed it to Sentry on June 3, 2026. According to Tenet, Sentry's leadership acknowledged the findings but declined a root-cause fix, describing the broader issue as "technically not defensible" [reference:16]. Tenet identified 2,388 organizations with publicly exposed Sentry credentials that could be used to inject malicious events at scale [reference:17].

**Why it should bother you:** this is supply chain risk, developer tooling risk, and AI agent trust risk, all stacked into one exploit. Your AI coding agents are now privileged insiders with a trust relationship to external services. Audit those relationships. Lock down public DSNs. Monitor developer endpoints like they're production.

